WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.750
- So today I'm going to do a few things.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:12.180
So usually start with
a bit of a motivation

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:15.040
to explain the broader
project in which this all fits

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:17.970
and what is the motivation behind it,

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:21.273
what is the open research
agenda on political lotteries?

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:23.430
Then the initial research

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.820
that Ali presented fits in
perfectly as an example,

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.280
so I won't go over that again,

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.430
but I will talk instead more
about this initial research

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:35.550
from an experiment on
online citizen assemblies,

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:37.140
which is a project coauthored

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.660
with Dominik Duell at the
University of Innsbruck

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.303
and Anam Kuraishi at the
University of Antwerp.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:47.310
So I don't need to go
into too much detail here

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:48.420
about the motivation.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:52.050
We all know that these experiments
with citizen assemblies

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.180
whereby randomly drawn citizens

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.950
are brought together to deliberate

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:58.380
are growing across the world
in different countries,

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.000
are gaining an importance.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.490
And the question is what
we can learn about that.

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:05.340
A particular topic in which
they've been organized is

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:09.090
climate and how we can
address climate change.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:11.640
There are lots of calls
for and also experiments

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.940
with these randomly drawn
citizens assemblies,

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:18.240
but also institutionalizing
them into more permanent

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.910
chambers of parliaments.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:23.340
And so with that shift

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.890
where these groups are
sometimes given legislative

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:29.760
initiative or at least
decision making powers

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.050
comes an important question.

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:33.330
And that is, what about the parties?

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:36.630
What about the sort of natural

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:38.520
ideological leanings
that people will have?

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:43.320
And what are the incentives
there to build parties?

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.200
And the question then becomes
whether all these advantages

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.350
that we know exist from
random citizen assemblies

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:52.710
can easily travel to
such a legislative arena

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:54.690
where we know that stakes are very high

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:57.360
and party affiliations come into play.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.060
The broad and general research question

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:02.640
of the broader project
is not just the better,

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.420
but really how can lotteries
as part of legislative

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:10.200
or democratic institutions
widen access to governance

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:14.583
to different minorities and
support equality in democracies.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:19.590
And so when people talk about
democracy and lotteries,

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.530
they often go back, way back in time.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:26.850
Same picture here as we saw
before today of the Kleroterion,

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.640
which if I remember well ,
I saw actually in Athens,

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.820
you can still see it in the
Museum of the Agora maybe,

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:36.273
of course you can correct me if I'm wrong.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:42.060
And so people go back to this of course

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.680
very prominent example,

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.660
we have a lot of recent work

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.450
that is very fascinating
on this particular case.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:52.230
But we know that lotteries were
used in different scenarios.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:56.100
Italian city republics
are very often mentioned

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.080
between the 14th and the 17th century.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.630
But there are others, such
as in Spain, in England,

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:05.253
and in Switzerland at a local
level and many others still.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.450
Lotteries can be found as examples as part

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:10.710
of democratic government

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:13.620
and Republican government
way back in time.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:16.080
And there's also some
recent work which gives

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:17.910
a lot of examples on this,

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:21.993
mainly theoretically or from
a broader historical overview.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:26.040
So lotteries were often used
to select office holders,

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:27.870
but then the story goes,

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:29.310
mass elections took over

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:32.606
as more people were getting involved

00:03:32.606 --> 00:03:33.656
as this was upscaled.

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:38.850
And so from lotteries,
democracies moved to elections

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:42.330
to organize the way to
select office holders.

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:45.250
And for a lot of people
that are thinking about

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:48.663
what is maybe going wrong
with democracy today,

00:03:49.732 --> 00:03:52.110
this element of citizen
participation that was there

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.873
by lotteries is what is
really missing today.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.620
But what is not often known

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:00.240
or what is less well known is

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.850
that lottery did not
immediately disappear.

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:05.820
And this story is an
important story to tell.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:08.550
It did not immediately
disappear in modern democracy.

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:10.590
In fact, they were used extensively

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:12.450
throughout democratizing Europe.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:15.158
And we don't know much about them

00:04:15.158 --> 00:04:17.250
in our empirical democratization studies.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:19.890
And so addressing that
is part of this project

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:22.950
that I will be working
on in the next five years

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:25.290
together with Ali, of course.

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:27.360
So the questions that I ask is,

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.300
did lotteries really widen
access to minorities?

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.030
And if they did so, how?

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:36.390
Was it maybe the case that
where lotteries were used,

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:39.630
it really enabled
constitution drafting given

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:43.380
that such high stakes were
at play, constitutions,

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:45.750
the shift to democratic government,

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:47.400
which would have consequences

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:50.100
down the line for many years and centuries

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.753
as we know post hoc,

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:56.790
what was the interaction with
the party system development

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:58.530
which was happening at the same time?

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:01.890
So what was the interests
of these parties that were

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:04.320
emerging at the time, and
how did that structure,

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:06.020
the way that the lotteries worked,

00:05:07.503 --> 00:05:09.480
and are there maybe long-term consequences

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:12.720
for political inequality that
we've maybe underestimated

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:16.473
because we've sort of forgotten
these peculiar procedures.

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:20.760
So the methodology of
the project is historical

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:24.600
to check each natural
experiment separately

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:28.890
and then place that together
with online experiments today

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:31.440
in a comparative perspective.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:33.480
And you can think of examples

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:35.520
where this happened in one shot cases

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.160
as an example of Denmark we saw today,

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.710
but it also happened in
more modern legislatures

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:42.840
as we have some work on in the past

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:45.000
where this happened repeatedly over time.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.790
And it's of course particularly
there that you need

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:49.860
to think about party system dynamics

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:54.390
and the dynamics of using
lotteries repeatedly over time

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:58.833
and which actors have an interest
in capturing that process.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:04.170
So the example today is really
this constituent assembly

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:06.450
in Denmark where you had
lotteries determining

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:10.740
the different committees
who then met and discussed

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:12.990
and prepared the constitution

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:14.690
and the draft of the Constitution.

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:20.070
And the idea is to put all
these different examples

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:24.240
into a comparative
analysis to study the use,

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:26.230
also the reasons for the abolishment

00:06:27.108 --> 00:06:27.941
and the effects of

00:06:27.941 --> 00:06:29.610
what we call these
lottery based procedures

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:31.920
across countries and time.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:36.150
And so the important hypothesis
are sort of listed here.

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:40.020
One idea is that they are
really used in context,

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:41.280
I cannot emphasize this enough,

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:43.290
where conflict is really high.

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:46.410
Yeah, if conflict is not that important,

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:48.870
then there are better
and more efficient ways

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:51.240
of organizing political decision making.

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.880
So when there's high distrust,

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:56.940
when party systems are splintered,

00:06:56.940 --> 00:07:00.030
when it's not clear who the opposition is,

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:02.580
like who belongs to which party maybe,

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:04.500
and when you have huge, for example,

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:07.950
inequalities as you had in
the 19th century, of course,

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:12.420
this is a scenario where
lotteries might be used by actors

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:15.513
and might be successful, I
say might because it depends.

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:19.083
Then the second thing to study is

00:07:19.083 --> 00:07:20.220
to think about is whether these lotteries

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:21.420
really opened access

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:24.180
to the decision making
process as we assume

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:26.970
that lotteries do because
that's in their very nature.

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:28.620
By randomly drawing individuals,

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:31.383
you equalize opportunity of access to all,

00:07:32.475 --> 00:07:34.530
you strengthen the
minorities who may otherwise

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:36.720
be easily ignored.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:40.230
So the question there is whether
when you open this access,

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:42.840
are there long-term consequences?

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:45.600
Does that work by leveling
political inequality

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:47.700
in political selection, for example?

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:50.220
Does it work through
the types of decisions

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:52.260
that are made in this legislature?

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:55.593
And how does that translate
into evolution over time?

00:07:56.520 --> 00:08:00.570
So the three main hypotheses
are summarized here.

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:05.570
So first, when it's used in
highly conflictual situations,

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:10.410
it might be precisely useful
for enabling collaboration

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:12.033
to exist in the first place.

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:14.640
So for example,

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:17.340
the procedures that we used
in this one-shot fashion

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:18.840
in constituent assemblies,

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:22.530
it might enable party
system development over time

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:24.510
because you have a procedure in place

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:26.130
while these parties emerge.

00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:29.700
And thirdly, once these
parties become stronger,

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:31.860
they will prefer a more
efficient procedure

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:36.060
and they might move to
abolish these procedures.

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:38.670
That you can test by looking at these

00:08:38.670 --> 00:08:42.510
procedures in repeated
legislative assemblies.

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:45.180
It's important to understand
the long-term effects

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:47.490
on political inequality from this broader

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:50.640
academic point of view

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:52.930
to understand what is really going on

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:57.270
in the long term when
we use such procedures.

00:08:57.270 --> 00:09:00.360
So method wise, it's a
lot of data collection

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:01.650
that hasn't been done before.

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:05.550
So it's using information
on 19th century legislators,

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:08.874
the way they voted, the
characteristics they had,

00:09:08.874 --> 00:09:11.400
sometimes they weren't
even in formalized parties,

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:12.240
et cetera.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:15.210
And then for each natural experiment,

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:19.170
the idea is to analyze
each lottery in country

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:22.830
and over time to really
check whether it increased

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:25.530
the diversity of the people involved

00:09:25.530 --> 00:09:28.053
in the political debates, did it do that?

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:30.480
And then secondly,

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:32.670
can we show that there
are any long-term effects,

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:34.740
either because it affected the votes

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:36.940
as you saw in the Denmark
case this morning,

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:41.040
or because it enabled
party system development,

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:44.100
or because it affected
long-term inequality.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:49.100
Now some of these lotteries can
of course be a bit limiting,

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:50.460
which is why we also want

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:53.670
to implement this in
online experiments, why?

00:09:53.670 --> 00:09:58.290
Because we can control more
of the aspects of lotteries.

00:09:58.290 --> 00:10:00.270
We can of course not go
back in time in history

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:01.620
and change these things,

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:04.290
but to check really whether the idea

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:06.600
that certain mechanisms are going on

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:10.080
also receive support in experiments today.

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:13.200
And I'm finally putting this
all together in a comparative

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:18.003
analysis to inform successful
lottery design today.

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:21.930
And I've given this
talk a number of times,

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:25.080
so I know that you're
going to tell me, but why,

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:26.880
why does this matter?

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.610
So lessons from lotteries to today,

00:10:29.610 --> 00:10:31.113
why does it matter for today?

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:34.530
If you think about the example

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:36.840
that Ali presented this morning,

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:38.520
you have elected legislators being

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:40.830
randomly assigned to different committees.

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:45.830
And what we saw there was very
little room for deliberation

00:10:46.902 --> 00:10:49.140
to really have a strong effect.

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:52.200
But at the same time,
Denmark wrote a constitution,

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:54.420
and the constitution
remains in place today.

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:57.330
So one comment is, well,

00:10:57.330 --> 00:10:59.700
elected legislators are very different

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:01.323
from ordinary citizens.

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:05.250
In this constituent assembly,

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:08.850
you have a binding
decision, very high stakes,

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:12.480
you have huge inequalities and conflict,

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:14.010
low levels of trust.

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:17.280
But consensus is really important here.

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:20.010
You will not achieve a
constitution unless you all,

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:22.020
unless this is a broadly
supported process,

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:23.700
you really need consensus.

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:26.190
All the actors involved regardless of

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:30.993
their particular stance on
specific items of this question.

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:34.890
And so this may be very different

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:37.050
from citizen assemblies today.

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:39.570
It may be a particularly hard case

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:42.330
to discover any deliberation effects

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:47.330
because we know that partisan
beliefs are important here.

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:51.330
But actually I would
argue it's very similar

00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:55.050
to today's problem about
climate change mitigation,

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:58.770
where you also have huge
inequalities between actors.

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:00.900
Because addressing climate change

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:04.920
will have huge
redistributive consequences.

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:08.470
It's a scenario where a
consensus is very important

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:12.120
for a collective action where
this needs to be a decision

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:15.390
supported by a lot of different actors.

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:16.980
And so we have lessons

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:20.370
to learn from these
legislative assemblies,

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:23.940
lottery holds promises for
this deliberation to work,

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:25.260
but we need to take serious

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:27.390
that the redistributive effects

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:28.920
may encourage strategic behavior.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:30.780
And you see it in some
ways in the backlash

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:34.710
that some of these assemblies
have had in the past.

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:36.930
So I wanted to give you this overview

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:39.330
to let you know what the project is about,

00:12:39.330 --> 00:12:42.210
but also to give a good introduction

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:45.990
of why I will now present
a slightly peculiar,

00:12:45.990 --> 00:12:49.020
interesting hopefully, experiment.

00:12:49.020 --> 00:12:52.290
So the idea was to model a
present day citizen assembly

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:55.350
in a very abstract way,

00:12:55.350 --> 00:12:57.870
but by introducing financial incentives

00:12:57.870 --> 00:13:00.900
as you would in a behavioral experiment,

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:02.520
to really understand,

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:04.350
to be able to predict what should happen,

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:05.730
and to really understand

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:08.193
what the mechanism is in different steps.

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:12.843
Here in particular, to
introduce strategic behavior.

00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:16.473
So I will go onto that now.

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:19.590
So how do people deliberate

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:23.190
and decide in strategic environments?

00:13:23.190 --> 00:13:26.823
Here we apply this to climate
change, collective action.

00:13:27.692 --> 00:13:29.910
I mentioned how it's
relevant and important

00:13:29.910 --> 00:13:31.290
because of high stakes

00:13:31.290 --> 00:13:34.230
and because of the promises
that lotteries give us here

00:13:34.230 --> 00:13:36.660
for randomly drawn decision makers.

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:38.910
There are two aspects in this experiment

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:40.560
that are really important for me

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:43.800
in structuring the exercise.

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:46.140
One is that this is a group decision.

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:48.920
A lot of work has not thought
about the fact that this,

00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:51.270
or I mean experimental work, I should say,

00:13:51.270 --> 00:13:52.980
is a group decision.

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:56.550
And secondly, costs, costs
are also very important.

00:13:56.550 --> 00:13:59.490
When you think about citizen assemblies,

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:02.430
you need to think about
how people interpret this

00:14:02.430 --> 00:14:06.420
in terms of if has real costs for them.

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:07.950
So in this experiment,

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:11.310
people are allowed to
share if they want to.

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:12.510
It's voluntary.

00:14:12.510 --> 00:14:14.370
They're allowed to share their beliefs

00:14:14.370 --> 00:14:18.060
and opinions in an
online group discussion.

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:21.690
But I'm controlling certain
parameters of this interaction

00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:24.690
to really understand what you
should theoretically predict

00:14:24.690 --> 00:14:29.103
or should theoretically
find in such a setup.

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:31.200
And the initial results

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:32.970
that I will tell you about today come

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:35.130
from a pilot experiment that I run

00:14:35.130 --> 00:14:37.710
where we find that if you tell people

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:40.020
before going into the
deliberation that a vote

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:42.900
is going to come up
which is costly for them,

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:44.790
it changes the decision making process,

00:14:44.790 --> 00:14:47.290
it changes their behavior
and changes the outcome.

00:14:51.870 --> 00:14:55.380
So we know a lot about deliberation.

00:14:55.380 --> 00:14:56.520
We also know a lot about

00:14:56.520 --> 00:14:59.130
how people think about
addressing climate change

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:00.540
from existing literature.

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:02.100
And this is just a slide

00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:05.010
with three different important parts

00:15:05.010 --> 00:15:06.670
from the experimental literature

00:15:08.488 --> 00:15:10.590
on climate change deliberation.

00:15:10.590 --> 00:15:12.660
So first of all, an
experimental literature

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:15.960
on citizen assemblies and
within deliberative democracy

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:18.570
is positive, mostly positive,

00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:20.910
showing that individuals really learn,

00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:23.550
they really intensively deliberate,

00:15:23.550 --> 00:15:25.020
they pick up information,

00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:27.630
and they successfully
take collective action

00:15:27.630 --> 00:15:29.730
against climate change.

00:15:29.730 --> 00:15:32.460
There are a few studies
that find no results

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:36.393
or opposite effects, but
overwhelmingly positive evidence.

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:38.670
The thing with these assemblies is

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:40.710
that they're very often advisory.

00:15:40.710 --> 00:15:43.530
And so their decision that
they take is not costly

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:47.070
to the people involved,
not directly costly,

00:15:47.070 --> 00:15:49.380
because it's not binding,
because it's advisory,

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:52.023
and more things happen down the line.

00:15:53.833 --> 00:15:55.623
And what we also know
from this literature is

00:15:55.623 --> 00:15:58.260
that the way this conversation takes place

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:02.100
or the quality of this
conversation is really important.

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:04.620
So it has to be well moderated,

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:09.620
people have to be able to
really express their minds,

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:11.280
et cetera.

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:12.570
What the literature also shows is

00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:15.960
that if you increase the
complexity of this interaction,

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:19.620
or you ask people to take a
binding decision in a vote

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:20.883
or something like that,

00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:24.130
it becomes more difficult to achieve

00:16:25.647 --> 00:16:26.973
these beneficial outcomes.

00:16:27.900 --> 00:16:30.570
So if we make this decision binding,

00:16:30.570 --> 00:16:32.620
that's a scenario where we should really

00:16:34.020 --> 00:16:36.900
expect strategic behavior.

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:38.070
And this is a good model

00:16:38.070 --> 00:16:40.650
of thinking about new parties developing

00:16:40.650 --> 00:16:42.630
in such environments.

00:16:42.630 --> 00:16:44.580
The second part of the
literature that is relevant

00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:46.440
and important is literature

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:48.870
that looks at climate change mitigation.

00:16:48.870 --> 00:16:51.000
And when people support this,

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:55.740
they find a lot of people support
climate change mitigation.

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:57.780
But it really is conditional

00:16:57.780 --> 00:17:00.870
on how to what extent today
will be affected themselves.

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:05.550
So they're supportive as
long as the costs are low.

00:17:05.550 --> 00:17:06.780
And that is of course related

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:10.170
to their preexisting
economic vulnerability

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:15.170
to these changing measures,
to their ideology, altruism,

00:17:16.230 --> 00:17:19.230
egalitarian preferences, and so on.

00:17:19.230 --> 00:17:21.120
But what these studies

00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:26.010
and the next ones also
ignore is that in these types

00:17:26.010 --> 00:17:28.290
of decision making processes

00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:29.760
decisions are taken collectively.

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:31.320
So from behavioral experiments,

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:34.540
we also know that we can
think about climate change

00:17:35.687 --> 00:17:38.123
as a public bad and people
tend to underinvest in it.

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:42.210
So what is important for us,
given that we know all this,

00:17:42.210 --> 00:17:44.310
what is it that we really want to distill?

00:17:45.187 --> 00:17:47.070
One, it has to be a group decision.

00:17:47.070 --> 00:17:51.080
And the costs have to be substantively,

00:17:51.080 --> 00:17:52.588
there have to be costs over

00:17:52.588 --> 00:17:54.303
substantively meaningful choices.

00:17:55.380 --> 00:18:00.380
So I hope I've now sufficiently
motivated this experiment,

00:18:00.690 --> 00:18:02.910
and I will now go into the details

00:18:02.910 --> 00:18:04.623
and unleash the critiques.

00:18:05.730 --> 00:18:08.040
So our experiment was pre-registered.

00:18:08.040 --> 00:18:10.710
It's a pilot that took place in the lab

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:13.140
because it's a pilot and
because we understand

00:18:13.140 --> 00:18:15.900
and know that there are a
lot of difficulties involved

00:18:15.900 --> 00:18:18.780
with encouraging good deliberation online.

00:18:18.780 --> 00:18:21.630
But people came to the experimental lab

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:24.720
and were assigned a workstation

00:18:24.720 --> 00:18:27.210
and entered into an online deliberation

00:18:27.210 --> 00:18:28.800
with each other over Zoom.

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:32.940
It allowed us to control video,
sound quality, and so on.

00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:36.243
But they had an actual interaction online.

00:18:37.470 --> 00:18:39.600
It's an incentivized experiment,

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:41.700
which means that after eliciting

00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:44.130
their current carbon footprint,

00:18:44.130 --> 00:18:48.720
we try to mimic the real
costs in the lab of saying

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:51.360
that you will reduce your pollution,

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:52.920
your individual pollution.

00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:54.200
And then the question of course is,

00:18:54.200 --> 00:18:56.310
is this manipulation-proof?

00:18:56.310 --> 00:18:59.790
Will there be changes to
deliberation and to the outcomes?

00:18:59.790 --> 00:19:01.620
So the experiment is incentivized,

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:03.330
it's this type of experiment,

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:05.160
but it's not incentivized to stick

00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:08.130
to an original randomly
assigned viewpoint.

00:19:08.130 --> 00:19:09.120
That makes no sense

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:12.062
when you're talking about
these types of topics,

00:19:12.062 --> 00:19:14.940
you want people to really
express their held opinions

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:16.650
and beliefs about this.

00:19:16.650 --> 00:19:18.750
So people are allowed to do that.

00:19:18.750 --> 00:19:22.020
And what we randomly
assign is which individuals

00:19:22.020 --> 00:19:24.480
are appointed to which groups.

00:19:24.480 --> 00:19:27.090
So participants would come into the lab,

00:19:27.090 --> 00:19:31.083
they would draw a number, a
small little ball from a bag,

00:19:32.019 --> 00:19:32.970
and some of them would be sent home

00:19:32.970 --> 00:19:35.100
and some of them entered the experiments.

00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:38.910
So people are randomly
assigned to the groups

00:19:38.910 --> 00:19:40.503
and to the treatments as well.

00:19:41.730 --> 00:19:44.190
Then we use our existing
pollution behavior

00:19:44.190 --> 00:19:47.190
as they tell us it is to
stretch out this group

00:19:47.190 --> 00:19:48.690
on a scale of income points.

00:19:48.690 --> 00:19:50.400
So some stand to gain

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:53.430
or lose more than others
in financial incentives

00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:55.413
I mean in this experiment.

00:19:56.992 --> 00:19:58.560
The topic is of course also important

00:19:58.560 --> 00:20:00.690
because people have to
talk about something

00:20:00.690 --> 00:20:03.714
that is substantively meaningful to them.

00:20:03.714 --> 00:20:07.350
And here we decided to follow
the UK Climate Assembly

00:20:07.350 --> 00:20:10.650
and we used their materials
to make a really brief,

00:20:10.650 --> 00:20:15.000
really short distillation
of these materials

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:19.860
and I'll show you later
on either flights or food.

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:23.760
And that is not to say
at all that we think

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:26.580
that individuals can solve climate change

00:20:26.580 --> 00:20:29.520
by changing their own behavior.

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:32.554
But what we want is that this deliberation

00:20:32.554 --> 00:20:34.859
is substantively meaningful for them.

00:20:34.859 --> 00:20:37.020
And that they don't think
of these topics as well,

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:39.960
that's the government's
job, not saying it isn't.

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:42.330
I'm saying it's important
that they deliberate

00:20:42.330 --> 00:20:45.600
over something that they
can actually control.

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:48.090
That the costs are meaningful
to them also as well.

00:20:48.090 --> 00:20:51.843
Not just a strange
payoff in an experiment.

00:20:53.010 --> 00:20:57.210
So how do people then
deliberate on collective action

00:20:57.210 --> 00:20:58.863
about climate change?

00:21:00.300 --> 00:21:03.960
So this is the basic
structure of the experiment.

00:21:03.960 --> 00:21:06.870
Groups of seven were recruited

00:21:06.870 --> 00:21:08.760
and they first fill out a survey

00:21:08.760 --> 00:21:11.130
to elicit their climate policy preferences

00:21:11.130 --> 00:21:14.477
after of course consent
and information forms.

00:21:14.477 --> 00:21:17.100
And then they told us about
their current carbon footprint.

00:21:17.100 --> 00:21:18.690
We took that and we assigned them

00:21:18.690 --> 00:21:20.640
to income points two to six.

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:23.130
And we told them, this is your endowment.

00:21:23.130 --> 00:21:26.340
And every two points
is worth an extra pound

00:21:26.340 --> 00:21:29.403
in this experiment, took place in the UK.

00:21:30.300 --> 00:21:32.880
Now with some probability, however,

00:21:32.880 --> 00:21:34.770
climate catastrophe will happen,

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:38.226
and then you'll receive only
one point of that endowment.

00:21:38.226 --> 00:21:40.833
Catastrophe happens.
It's bad for everyone.

00:21:41.670 --> 00:21:44.730
So how much tax are you now willing to pay

00:21:44.730 --> 00:21:48.960
to avoid this catastrophe
wipes out these earnings.

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:53.520
That's what we call the
pre-deliberation platform.

00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:56.163
There's a control group
who doesn't talk at all.

00:21:57.360 --> 00:21:58.830
There is a treatment group

00:21:58.830 --> 00:22:01.860
that goes into deliberation over Zoom.

00:22:01.860 --> 00:22:03.990
Because that has to be incentivized too.

00:22:03.990 --> 00:22:07.230
They have to come up with a
group statement afterwards.

00:22:07.230 --> 00:22:10.110
And there's a strategic
liberation treatment

00:22:10.110 --> 00:22:13.080
where the only difference
is that we first tell them,

00:22:13.080 --> 00:22:15.330
oh, the next stage is
coming up, by the way.

00:22:15.330 --> 00:22:18.330
And that's a vote. Next stage is the vote.

00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:20.692
Everyone is told about the vote

00:22:20.692 --> 00:22:22.263
if they hadn't been told before.

00:22:22.263 --> 00:22:26.160
And when they vote, we take
this tax that they vote for

00:22:26.160 --> 00:22:28.530
from this endowment that we gave them.

00:22:28.530 --> 00:22:32.160
We also realize the catastrophe
with some probability,

00:22:32.160 --> 00:22:34.200
so that if they didn't invest enough,

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:38.070
they have a higher risk of
their earnings being wiped out.

00:22:38.070 --> 00:22:40.440
And then we do other
things that are typical

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:44.220
of behavioral experiments
to elicit willingness

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:46.830
to cooperate risk preferences, et cetera

00:22:46.830 --> 00:22:49.230
and to make sure we have measures of that

00:22:49.230 --> 00:22:51.603
to control for these things.

00:22:53.460 --> 00:22:56.280
Okay, where are the
incentives coming from?

00:22:56.280 --> 00:22:57.960
After reading the information,

00:22:57.960 --> 00:23:00.750
but before the deliberation and voting,

00:23:00.750 --> 00:23:03.333
we ask them, what do you think,

00:23:05.432 --> 00:23:07.620
which of these five things
do you think should happen?

00:23:07.620 --> 00:23:08.453
Nothing.

00:23:09.788 --> 00:23:13.533
Low tax or high or the
highest possible tax.

00:23:13.533 --> 00:23:16.200
And now we show them, if
these were a real vote,

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:18.150
this is what the outcome would've been.

00:23:19.050 --> 00:23:21.810
So this experiment, it's
important to point out,

00:23:21.810 --> 00:23:25.380
has a common value by design,
like we designed it that way.

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:27.840
We wanted people to pick a higher tax

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:31.470
because it's protective
against climate change.

00:23:31.470 --> 00:23:34.653
It's an insurance, you can
think of it for everyone.

00:23:36.270 --> 00:23:40.740
The control group has no
discussion, but before the vote,

00:23:40.740 --> 00:23:42.360
that's also important to mention,

00:23:42.360 --> 00:23:47.280
everyone knows the distribution
of points at least.

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:49.800
And then finally, an
important note to make is

00:23:49.800 --> 00:23:53.073
that one moderator was present,

00:23:54.160 --> 00:23:56.760
but was purposely asked to stay neutral.

00:23:56.760 --> 00:23:59.700
So the only thing they did
was say, well, this is work,

00:23:59.700 --> 00:24:01.200
I'm keeping time.

00:24:01.200 --> 00:24:02.670
Maybe they would say,

00:24:02.670 --> 00:24:04.170
perhaps you would like to contribute.

00:24:04.170 --> 00:24:07.323
And at the end, please
write up a group statement.

00:24:08.970 --> 00:24:10.740
What would you expect to see?

00:24:10.740 --> 00:24:14.610
Well a greater willingness
to choose a higher tax

00:24:14.610 --> 00:24:16.380
from the deliberation effect,

00:24:16.380 --> 00:24:19.210
that's what you expect
from these different

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:21.690
theories on deliberation.

00:24:21.690 --> 00:24:26.370
And that might be because
their attention is drawn

00:24:26.370 --> 00:24:30.960
by reading this information
because they gained information

00:24:30.960 --> 00:24:32.730
or because they're persuaded by some

00:24:32.730 --> 00:24:34.880
of the individuals that
they're talking to.

00:24:35.790 --> 00:24:38.343
Deliberations were very
short, 10 minutes only.

00:24:39.720 --> 00:24:42.000
The hypothesis on strategic

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:44.460
deliberation are somewhat different.

00:24:44.460 --> 00:24:46.920
So if you know about
the upcoming election,

00:24:46.920 --> 00:24:50.160
you introduce incentives
to change this process

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:53.550
because it might be
better for you financially

00:24:53.550 --> 00:24:56.040
if you have a different type of outcome.

00:24:56.040 --> 00:24:57.960
So it'll change the nature
of the deliberation.

00:24:57.960 --> 00:24:59.730
And then in our pre-registered experiment,

00:24:59.730 --> 00:25:02.070
we had a number of more
detailed hypotheses,

00:25:02.070 --> 00:25:04.070
but I won't go into that too much today.

00:25:05.490 --> 00:25:09.000
So first is the control group.

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:12.240
And remember we have this
entry voting and outcome stage.

00:25:12.240 --> 00:25:14.070
We expect to see an outcome

00:25:14.070 --> 00:25:16.980
that deliberating groups more often vote

00:25:16.980 --> 00:25:19.530
for a higher tax as you would see in lots

00:25:19.530 --> 00:25:21.960
of these other experiments
taking place across the world.

00:25:21.960 --> 00:25:23.760
But in a strategic deliberation,

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:25.920
you should see that they would choose

00:25:25.920 --> 00:25:27.540
for either of these two options,

00:25:27.540 --> 00:25:30.720
which means that the
total effect is noisy.

00:25:30.720 --> 00:25:33.469
So this positive deliberation effect

00:25:33.469 --> 00:25:37.533
is sort of decreased
somehow, is disturbed,

00:25:38.652 --> 00:25:40.440
and the overall effect,
well we can't know.

00:25:40.440 --> 00:25:42.510
And then the mechanism is
something we're still working on.

00:25:42.510 --> 00:25:46.110
We're analyzing what people
said in the conversation,

00:25:46.110 --> 00:25:50.220
the quality of the deliberation
to sort of really test

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:53.040
which of these three
mechanisms can explain

00:25:53.040 --> 00:25:54.393
the deliberation effects.

00:25:57.000 --> 00:26:00.540
Should I go over the
sequence of moves once again?

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:03.480
Because I know it's a lot of things.

00:26:03.480 --> 00:26:07.023
Also happy to come back to this.

00:26:07.980 --> 00:26:12.570
These were the materials,
for example, on flying first,

00:26:12.570 --> 00:26:14.550
a small bit, very structured.

00:26:14.550 --> 00:26:16.710
This is how much flying matters.

00:26:16.710 --> 00:26:20.010
Secondly, technological
improvements might happen,

00:26:20.010 --> 00:26:21.120
but that's uncertain.

00:26:21.120 --> 00:26:22.500
And then finally,

00:26:22.500 --> 00:26:26.310
you can do something by
reducing your demand.

00:26:26.310 --> 00:26:30.180
Same for food. Food is
important in pollution.

00:26:30.180 --> 00:26:33.480
Technological improvements
may happen and solve things,

00:26:33.480 --> 00:26:37.260
but demand from consumers may grow.

00:26:37.260 --> 00:26:39.600
So you can do something
by reducing your demand.

00:26:39.600 --> 00:26:40.653
Very structured.

00:26:42.930 --> 00:26:44.010
Some initial results.

00:26:44.010 --> 00:26:47.160
So this is the number of groups we had

00:26:47.160 --> 00:26:51.150
and the number of people, 378 in total.

00:26:51.150 --> 00:26:53.490
And the distribution across deliberation,

00:26:53.490 --> 00:26:56.223
strategic deliberation,
and a control group.

00:26:57.540 --> 00:27:02.190
At the top, you see the
distribution in carbon footprints

00:27:02.190 --> 00:27:04.980
we forced them into, and underlying that,

00:27:04.980 --> 00:27:07.650
you see the distribution
in carbon footprints

00:27:07.650 --> 00:27:10.230
they actually had in the lab.

00:27:10.230 --> 00:27:11.490
So you see it's not ideal.

00:27:11.490 --> 00:27:14.070
We would've preferred better distribution,

00:27:14.070 --> 00:27:15.640
but it's also not that bad

00:27:17.266 --> 00:27:19.230
in terms of you can see that
group six pollutes more,

00:27:19.230 --> 00:27:21.513
has a higher footprint than group two.

00:27:23.640 --> 00:27:26.640
This is what they told us,
their initial preferences.

00:27:26.640 --> 00:27:31.260
So if they cared only about
the financial incentive,

00:27:31.260 --> 00:27:32.520
you wouldn't see this,

00:27:32.520 --> 00:27:33.750
you would see very clearly

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:37.470
that the people on the
carbon footprint six

00:27:37.470 --> 00:27:38.730
would say do nothing.

00:27:38.730 --> 00:27:40.740
And people on carbon footprint two

00:27:40.740 --> 00:27:44.400
would say highest reduction,
purely financially,

00:27:44.400 --> 00:27:45.990
purely based on financial incentives.

00:27:45.990 --> 00:27:48.840
But you don't see this.
You see a mix of things.

00:27:48.840 --> 00:27:50.370
What is important to note though,

00:27:50.370 --> 00:27:54.632
is that almost no one said,
in our sample at least,

00:27:54.632 --> 00:27:56.820
people that were in our lab,
that they want to do nothing.

00:27:56.820 --> 00:27:57.930
There were a few of those,

00:27:57.930 --> 00:28:00.420
but not a lot of people said do nothing.

00:28:00.420 --> 00:28:03.060
Quite a few, however, said lowest please

00:28:03.060 --> 00:28:06.453
or low reduction rather
than high or highest.

00:28:08.040 --> 00:28:09.960
And this is the results.

00:28:09.960 --> 00:28:14.940
So we see the blue dot here,
that deliberating groups

00:28:14.940 --> 00:28:17.220
more often vote for the high tax level.

00:28:17.220 --> 00:28:22.220
So a high or highest
level as group choices

00:28:22.350 --> 00:28:24.210
after having spoken to each other.

00:28:24.210 --> 00:28:26.100
But if we break that up between groups

00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:29.070
who are deliberating freely
and groups who are told

00:28:29.070 --> 00:28:30.750
before that there would be this vote

00:28:30.750 --> 00:28:33.690
and their earnings would be
affected in the experiment,

00:28:33.690 --> 00:28:35.550
you see a difference.

00:28:35.550 --> 00:28:38.520
You see that the deliberating
group is positive,

00:28:38.520 --> 00:28:41.760
strategic liberation is
less likely to support it.

00:28:41.760 --> 00:28:43.500
And the difference is significant,

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:46.530
the green line between these two

00:28:46.530 --> 00:28:48.130
different types of deliberation.

00:28:49.260 --> 00:28:53.870
The final thing I want to show
you is I talked a bit about,

00:28:53.870 --> 00:28:57.540
we also checked entry
and voting and outcomes.

00:28:57.540 --> 00:29:00.153
So this was outcome, this was
the outcome of these groups.

00:29:00.153 --> 00:29:02.190
This is at group level.

00:29:02.190 --> 00:29:04.440
Here we want to look
at individuals voting.

00:29:04.440 --> 00:29:07.683
So among these 370 people,

00:29:08.640 --> 00:29:13.590
did they changed their vote
from their original preferences?

00:29:13.590 --> 00:29:17.460
And we don't see that if
we leave it unrestricted

00:29:17.460 --> 00:29:19.560
in columns one, two, three.

00:29:19.560 --> 00:29:22.170
But if we ask, did they
change their votes away from

00:29:22.170 --> 00:29:24.300
what they were initially saying

00:29:24.300 --> 00:29:27.450
they would choose to either
a high or a highest tax level

00:29:27.450 --> 00:29:30.780
as we encourage them to say,

00:29:30.780 --> 00:29:34.350
or as the experiment was
designed to encourage them to do,

00:29:34.350 --> 00:29:36.330
we do see that it's more likely

00:29:36.330 --> 00:29:40.200
that those in the deliberation
treatment move there

00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:43.530
and those in a strategic
deliberation less so

00:29:43.530 --> 00:29:44.970
restricting the sample of course

00:29:44.970 --> 00:29:46.860
to people who did not already have

00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:49.260
the highest preference in the first place,

00:29:49.260 --> 00:29:51.663
because they cannot be
influenced any further.

00:29:53.850 --> 00:29:58.260
So to conclude, compared to the baseline,

00:29:58.260 --> 00:30:00.390
and compared to each other,

00:30:00.390 --> 00:30:03.960
we see differences between
the type of deliberation.

00:30:03.960 --> 00:30:06.570
Deliberation has great outcomes,

00:30:06.570 --> 00:30:10.620
but less so when the
deliberation is strategic.

00:30:10.620 --> 00:30:12.750
The next step is to analyze the quality

00:30:12.750 --> 00:30:13.860
of these deliberations

00:30:13.860 --> 00:30:16.140
and to say something about why do we see

00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:17.940
this positive effect and what is it

00:30:17.940 --> 00:30:19.740
that these people told us in this

00:30:19.740 --> 00:30:22.950
or each other, not us directly,

00:30:22.950 --> 00:30:25.531
into why they cared about us

00:30:25.531 --> 00:30:27.330
and why a decision was so important.

00:30:27.330 --> 00:30:29.610
And this is just preliminary

00:30:29.610 --> 00:30:31.140
because it's only for parts

00:30:31.140 --> 00:30:33.450
of the sample suite, might change.

00:30:33.450 --> 00:30:36.150
But we do see that the
quality of the deliberation

00:30:36.150 --> 00:30:38.520
as typically measured in the literature

00:30:38.520 --> 00:30:41.280
was higher among the non-strategic group

00:30:41.280 --> 00:30:43.780
done in a strategic group
on a number of different

00:30:44.670 --> 00:30:46.710
arguments such as neutral arguments,

00:30:46.710 --> 00:30:51.033
neutral counter arguments,
no interruptions, et cetera.

00:30:53.160 --> 00:30:55.500
So in the future,

00:30:55.500 --> 00:30:58.930
the plan is to build this
up and to scale this up

00:30:59.790 --> 00:31:02.160
either by potentially
bringing these people together

00:31:02.160 --> 00:31:04.650
in a larger group and then seeing

00:31:04.650 --> 00:31:08.190
what eventually happens
in this larger assembly,

00:31:08.190 --> 00:31:10.320
or to dig in more detail

00:31:10.320 --> 00:31:13.230
and improve the tests of
the deliberation mechanism.

00:31:13.230 --> 00:31:15.600
Another question that I often have

00:31:15.600 --> 00:31:17.300
and that I'm still thinking about,

00:31:18.247 --> 00:31:20.288
I'm happy to talk about today,

00:31:20.288 --> 00:31:22.380
is whether you need to
improve the real life analogy

00:31:22.380 --> 00:31:23.400
of these experiments.

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:24.330
A lot of people say, well,

00:31:24.330 --> 00:31:28.920
deliberation can just not
be measured in 10 minutes.

00:31:28.920 --> 00:31:32.880
I say, well these results
actually show that you can,

00:31:32.880 --> 00:31:34.410
I mean there clearly is a difference,

00:31:34.410 --> 00:31:37.920
even from as little as 10 minutes.

00:31:37.920 --> 00:31:39.870
So I'm still thinking about this part.

00:31:39.870 --> 00:31:43.689
I'm not saying it's not
important. Thank you.

00:31:43.689 --> 00:31:45.939
(applause)

00:32:00.450 --> 00:32:01.470
- [Ali] So this is neat,

00:32:01.470 --> 00:32:04.530
and I actually think you
have more in the qualitative,

00:32:04.530 --> 00:32:06.873
like the transcripts of the deliberation,

00:32:07.950 --> 00:32:08.880
or you could learn a lot.

00:32:08.880 --> 00:32:12.060
And so my question would
be in the strategic group,

00:32:12.060 --> 00:32:14.400
do you see evidence of, okay,

00:32:14.400 --> 00:32:16.080
I have to convince everyone to agree,

00:32:16.080 --> 00:32:18.480
so I know I'm not gonna get the tails.

00:32:18.480 --> 00:32:22.800
Like we're gonna have to
come up with a happy medium,

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:24.960
or do you see more bullying
in the strategic group?

00:32:24.960 --> 00:32:28.680
Does anyone say like, well,
I'm gonna be hurt the most,

00:32:28.680 --> 00:32:30.210
but okay, I'll compromise

00:32:30.210 --> 00:32:32.010
so we can come to a coordination,

00:32:32.010 --> 00:32:34.110
like the language and the tactics

00:32:34.110 --> 00:32:35.640
that people use to deliberate,

00:32:35.640 --> 00:32:39.240
can you look at the text of
the groups and see differences?

00:32:39.240 --> 00:32:40.530
Like what do you see?

00:32:40.530 --> 00:32:43.110
- So thank you Ali.

00:32:43.110 --> 00:32:44.360
That's a really good one.

00:32:45.570 --> 00:32:49.380
So we're looking at this by using the DQI,

00:32:49.380 --> 00:32:50.940
as is common in the literature,

00:32:50.940 --> 00:32:52.380
but we're also looking at

00:32:52.380 --> 00:32:56.493
whether they've used keywords
such as election, majority,

00:32:58.530 --> 00:33:00.660
most important preference,
things like that.

00:33:00.660 --> 00:33:02.910
And you know, from a first glimpse,

00:33:02.910 --> 00:33:04.200
not everything has been coded yet,

00:33:04.200 --> 00:33:05.700
but it does seem to be the case that

00:33:05.700 --> 00:33:10.050
that more often is part of
the language, for example.

00:33:10.050 --> 00:33:12.423
So yeah, that's an important one.

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:28.417
- [Dmitri] Thank you. Dmitri Landon, NYU.

00:33:28.417 --> 00:33:30.570
It seems like the strategic
deliberation treatment,

00:33:30.570 --> 00:33:32.550
kind of the plausibility of
the strategic deliberation

00:33:32.550 --> 00:33:34.980
treatment kind of relies on the subjects

00:33:34.980 --> 00:33:36.423
buying into the frame.

00:33:37.770 --> 00:33:42.150
And so my question is, do you
have exit interview questions

00:33:42.150 --> 00:33:46.410
that give us a sense of whether
they did buy into it or not?

00:33:46.410 --> 00:33:49.980
How much did the frame
ultimately matter to them?

00:33:49.980 --> 00:33:52.263
- Did you say exit survey?
- Yes.

00:33:54.990 --> 00:33:57.000
- I'm not sure it really answers

00:33:57.000 --> 00:33:58.260
this particular question.

00:33:58.260 --> 00:34:00.960
We did have an exit survey actually

00:34:00.960 --> 00:34:02.490
that we did not incentivize

00:34:02.490 --> 00:34:05.193
as is common in citizen
assemblies as well.

00:34:06.681 --> 00:34:07.800
We did not find any differences.

00:34:07.800 --> 00:34:10.860
People did not tell us that
they were more trustworthy

00:34:10.860 --> 00:34:14.883
or that they were happy
with the process, et cetera.

00:34:21.930 --> 00:34:23.250
- [Dmitri] What I mean is

00:34:23.250 --> 00:34:26.400
did your subjects really
understand themselves

00:34:26.400 --> 00:34:30.600
as making a choice with respect
to climate change policy?

00:34:30.600 --> 00:34:35.220
Or did they understand
themselves as just playing a game

00:34:35.220 --> 00:34:38.670
that has, as you said, there is like,

00:34:38.670 --> 00:34:39.900
I don't know if it's the dominant strategy

00:34:39.900 --> 00:34:41.800
in the game or not, but whatever it is

00:34:43.200 --> 00:34:44.463
to choose a high tax.

00:34:45.311 --> 00:34:46.144
So that's the question.

00:34:46.144 --> 00:34:48.690
If they did understand
themselves as really

00:34:48.690 --> 00:34:51.090
choosing a climate change policy,

00:34:51.090 --> 00:34:54.309
presumably we would interpret
these results somewhat

00:34:54.309 --> 00:34:56.400
differently than if we
thought this is just

00:34:57.287 --> 00:34:59.010
a diverting exercise.

00:34:59.010 --> 00:34:59.843
- That reminds me,

00:34:59.843 --> 00:35:01.780
I believe we also asked about

00:35:03.600 --> 00:35:06.240
if you're now thinking
about implementing this

00:35:06.240 --> 00:35:08.010
really into your own life,

00:35:08.010 --> 00:35:11.100
how would you think about reducing,

00:35:11.100 --> 00:35:13.020
what are sort of the efforts you can make?

00:35:13.020 --> 00:35:16.923
If I'm not mistaken, it was
one of our exit questions too.

00:35:18.390 --> 00:35:22.083
But can't tell you yet
what it was exactly.

00:35:23.160 --> 00:35:24.810
- [Dmitri] Another question I have is,

00:35:24.810 --> 00:35:27.330
have you thought about
the possibility of a joint

00:35:27.330 --> 00:35:30.900
payoff version of the
strategic deliberation game?

00:35:30.900 --> 00:35:33.330
So you have a binding policy choice,

00:35:33.330 --> 00:35:36.300
but their payoffs are
individual private payoffs.

00:35:36.300 --> 00:35:38.340
So one thing you might think about is

00:35:38.340 --> 00:35:40.473
having a joint payoff, group payoff,

00:35:42.030 --> 00:35:44.793
see how that alters the deliberation.

00:35:48.960 --> 00:35:52.173
- Well the tax that they were
paying is progressive though,

00:35:53.248 --> 00:35:58.248
so the higher polluters
are more intensively taxed

00:35:59.700 --> 00:36:02.973
in any case if the tax level
is that is chosen is higher.

00:36:03.960 --> 00:36:07.683
So it partly addresses that, not entirely.

00:36:09.510 --> 00:36:10.470
- [Dmitri] That's not quite--

00:36:10.470 --> 00:36:11.910
- We can talk about it.

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:12.743
- [Dmitri] If you think about,

00:36:12.743 --> 00:36:15.390
part of the value of
doing a joint payoff is

00:36:15.390 --> 00:36:17.370
that a joint payoff would
enable them to, again,

00:36:17.370 --> 00:36:20.610
see this as a more
cooperative kind of exercise.

00:36:20.610 --> 00:36:24.147
- But there's a lot going
on in the experiment,

00:36:24.147 --> 00:36:26.970
but we do have the shock
of climate catastrophe.

00:36:26.970 --> 00:36:29.730
So sometimes it happens
that if you choose a level

00:36:29.730 --> 00:36:33.240
of tax that is too low, you
get a draw, which is bad,

00:36:33.240 --> 00:36:36.240
and everyone's payments
are reduced to one.

00:36:36.240 --> 00:36:38.190
I mean, in this particular stage, yeah.

00:36:45.264 --> 00:36:46.440
- [Audience Member] I
think I just have a sort of

00:36:46.440 --> 00:36:47.733
an interpretive question.

00:36:48.630 --> 00:36:50.130
So you refer to it as a tax,

00:36:50.130 --> 00:36:53.370
but at one point you also
refer to it as insurance.

00:36:53.370 --> 00:36:55.560
Is it presented to them as insurance

00:36:55.560 --> 00:36:58.050
or is it presented to them as a tax?

00:36:58.050 --> 00:37:00.330
Partly I think this speaks

00:37:00.330 --> 00:37:02.790
to the collective dimensions

00:37:02.790 --> 00:37:05.103
of what they think they're doing.

00:37:06.052 --> 00:37:06.910
So you mentioned...

00:37:09.472 --> 00:37:12.870
You stressed that you
were not presupposing

00:37:12.870 --> 00:37:15.873
that there are individual
solutions to climate change.

00:37:17.010 --> 00:37:22.010
But I think that if you think
that you can buy insurance

00:37:22.230 --> 00:37:24.510
against climate change,

00:37:24.510 --> 00:37:29.510
that's precisely imposing a
kind of an individual solution,

00:37:31.600 --> 00:37:34.410
not in the sense of like,
I can solve climate change,

00:37:34.410 --> 00:37:36.390
but rather I can buy myself out

00:37:36.390 --> 00:37:38.250
of the consequences of climate change.

00:37:38.250 --> 00:37:41.190
I just wonder how you present it to them.

00:37:41.190 --> 00:37:43.847
- Experiments like that have been done.

00:37:43.847 --> 00:37:46.890
And what you see is the
typical free rider problem.

00:37:46.890 --> 00:37:49.410
It's very hard to get collective action.

00:37:49.410 --> 00:37:53.430
People tend to not put
enough into this common fund

00:37:53.430 --> 00:37:55.773
to protect and to buy investment.

00:37:56.610 --> 00:37:58.920
That's why it was so
important that they would talk

00:37:58.920 --> 00:38:00.990
to each other and make
a decision collectively,

00:38:00.990 --> 00:38:02.640
as in a vote,

00:38:02.640 --> 00:38:05.460
a vote which is decided by plurality rule,

00:38:05.460 --> 00:38:07.668
which is binding for everyone,

00:38:07.668 --> 00:38:09.383
also the people who did not vote for this.

00:38:10.650 --> 00:38:12.040
So I think that's precisely

00:38:12.949 --> 00:38:14.749
what the experiment tries to add to,

00:38:15.930 --> 00:38:18.180
experimental literature
is already out there.

00:38:25.920 --> 00:38:29.103
- [Brenda] Did you say you
had results on interruptions,

00:38:30.210 --> 00:38:31.503
who interrupts?

00:38:33.510 --> 00:38:34.833
Is it the men,

00:38:35.850 --> 00:38:36.900
because there's large literature,

00:38:36.900 --> 00:38:39.510
especially in like the parliamentary

00:38:39.510 --> 00:38:42.360
committees about like who
interrupts in senior politicians

00:38:42.360 --> 00:38:43.200
and gender,

00:38:43.200 --> 00:38:46.560
so who was interrupting and
did it vary across the groups

00:38:46.560 --> 00:38:48.510
and what were the interruption results?

00:38:50.730 --> 00:38:53.610
- So we're measuring this as
part of an item of the index.

00:38:53.610 --> 00:38:55.950
So we're looking at the
number of interactions.

00:38:55.950 --> 00:38:57.480
I often get this comment though,

00:38:57.480 --> 00:39:01.200
and I think it's absolutely important,

00:39:01.200 --> 00:39:04.060
but we randomly assigned our participants

00:39:05.016 --> 00:39:07.830
and we didn't really study
this aspect of the question,

00:39:07.830 --> 00:39:09.510
which is a very important one,

00:39:09.510 --> 00:39:12.982
but it's not one we really addressed.

00:39:12.982 --> 00:39:13.815
I mean, it's randomly assigned.

00:39:13.815 --> 00:39:16.500
We might look at whether
there are differences.

00:39:16.500 --> 00:39:18.180
We could do that.

00:39:18.180 --> 00:39:20.160
I mean in the final outcomes, right.

00:39:20.160 --> 00:39:22.530
Whether it affected the conversation,

00:39:22.530 --> 00:39:25.890
depending on these types
of characteristics as well.

00:39:25.890 --> 00:39:28.230
Even though when I look
at the characteristics

00:39:28.230 --> 00:39:30.900
of these groups, they
don't significantly differ

00:39:30.900 --> 00:39:32.820
in their composition on these types

00:39:32.820 --> 00:39:35.283
of factors that we collected.

00:39:37.147 --> 00:39:38.220
- [Brenda] I just meant in
general, like descriptively,

00:39:38.220 --> 00:39:39.240
like across the groups,

00:39:39.240 --> 00:39:42.810
do you see like one specific
type of person interrupt more?

00:39:42.810 --> 00:39:44.190
- Sometimes. I mean, yes.

00:39:44.190 --> 00:39:45.960
Like I can give you examples,

00:39:45.960 --> 00:39:47.910
but I don't know how systematic it is.

00:39:47.910 --> 00:39:49.593
This happens absolutely.

00:39:56.460 --> 00:39:58.021
- [Federica] Thank you, Brenda.

00:39:58.021 --> 00:39:59.613
Federica Carugati from KCL.

00:40:01.050 --> 00:40:02.130
This is just a clarification

00:40:02.130 --> 00:40:04.890
and it might be driven by
the fact that I was trying

00:40:04.890 --> 00:40:08.550
to like keep up with the
presentation of the whole project

00:40:08.550 --> 00:40:13.230
before you went into this
particular sort of a bit.

00:40:13.230 --> 00:40:15.820
And I'm wondering, did I
understand correctly that

00:40:16.659 --> 00:40:19.110
you are planning to look at historical

00:40:19.110 --> 00:40:22.320
and online experiments at the same time?

00:40:22.320 --> 00:40:23.790
And I'm really interested in that.

00:40:23.790 --> 00:40:26.814
And if you can just speak a
little bit more about like

00:40:26.814 --> 00:40:27.647
what's the hunch and what you expect

00:40:27.647 --> 00:40:29.850
to see in this comparative scenario,

00:40:29.850 --> 00:40:31.290
that'd be great, thank you.

00:40:31.290 --> 00:40:34.563
- Thank you, great question, thank you.

00:40:35.929 --> 00:40:38.100
I can get to be passionate
about the project.

00:40:38.100 --> 00:40:41.520
So for example, in the Denmark case

00:40:41.520 --> 00:40:44.460
that Ali presented this morning,
a comment we get a lot is,

00:40:44.460 --> 00:40:45.900
well these were five groups

00:40:45.900 --> 00:40:48.510
and what can you say from the fact

00:40:48.510 --> 00:40:50.550
that this happened only once.

00:40:50.550 --> 00:40:51.660
We say well a lot

00:40:51.660 --> 00:40:55.020
because this was a
really important context,

00:40:55.020 --> 00:40:59.460
a really consequential case,
and it should be studied.

00:40:59.460 --> 00:41:01.020
We shouldn't just pick it because

00:41:01.020 --> 00:41:02.643
it allows better statistics.

00:41:03.630 --> 00:41:06.360
But putting that into a
comparative perspective

00:41:06.360 --> 00:41:09.540
can really help us to draw
out some of these mechanisms

00:41:09.540 --> 00:41:12.840
that the historical cases do
not allow us to investigate.

00:41:12.840 --> 00:41:15.990
So does it matter how large the group is?

00:41:15.990 --> 00:41:20.220
Does it matter whether these
are seven people or 70 people?

00:41:20.220 --> 00:41:24.660
Does it matter by the types
of decision rule they use?

00:41:24.660 --> 00:41:27.600
As we know, as Ali said this
morning from electoral law

00:41:27.600 --> 00:41:29.910
should matter quite a lot

00:41:29.910 --> 00:41:31.660
for the different outcomes you get.

00:41:44.940 --> 00:41:47.250
- [Dmitri] Thank you, I want
to think a little bit more

00:41:47.250 --> 00:41:48.840
about the comparison of the kinds

00:41:48.840 --> 00:41:51.330
of arguments that you saw people use

00:41:51.330 --> 00:41:53.180
in the strategic liberation treatment

00:41:54.062 --> 00:41:55.920
in the non-strategic treatment.

00:41:55.920 --> 00:41:57.300
So in the strategic liberation treatment,

00:41:57.300 --> 00:41:58.890
you have a game, right?

00:41:58.890 --> 00:42:01.693
And in that game, I don't know, as I said,

00:42:01.693 --> 00:42:02.910
I don't see the payoffs.

00:42:02.910 --> 00:42:04.880
So it's a dominant strategy,

00:42:04.880 --> 00:42:08.283
is that right for them to
be preferring the high tax?

00:42:09.600 --> 00:42:11.114
Is it a dominant strategy for them

00:42:11.114 --> 00:42:13.378
to be preferring the high tax?

00:42:13.378 --> 00:42:14.760
- Yeah, I mean it's a trade off for them,

00:42:14.760 --> 00:42:17.490
in which the people who
are taxed more highly

00:42:17.490 --> 00:42:20.043
should be preferring a lower tax.

00:42:22.830 --> 00:42:26.250
- [Dmitri] Fine, but it's a game,

00:42:26.250 --> 00:42:29.370
and what they want to do is to incentivize

00:42:29.370 --> 00:42:34.370
their fellow players to
make one set of choices

00:42:34.380 --> 00:42:36.840
as opposed to another set of choices.

00:42:36.840 --> 00:42:38.460
And in that game, presumably,

00:42:38.460 --> 00:42:40.890
they're going to be using arguments

00:42:40.890 --> 00:42:42.720
that speak to their payoffs

00:42:42.720 --> 00:42:45.453
as you have given to them in the game.

00:42:46.830 --> 00:42:50.220
Whereas in the non-strategic treatment,

00:42:50.220 --> 00:42:53.280
that set of considerations
is off the table.

00:42:53.280 --> 00:42:57.054
The only relevant considerations
presumably, if any,

00:42:57.054 --> 00:42:58.980
are going to be frame
related considerations,

00:42:58.980 --> 00:43:00.810
which is to say it's climate change

00:43:00.810 --> 00:43:03.540
or do we support higher, lower,

00:43:03.540 --> 00:43:04.530
or sort of more aggressive

00:43:04.530 --> 00:43:07.530
or less aggressive policy in
response to climate change.

00:43:07.530 --> 00:43:09.240
So in some sense, it's not surprising

00:43:09.240 --> 00:43:12.510
that you will see very
different kinds of arguments

00:43:12.510 --> 00:43:13.810
that would be on the table

00:43:14.942 --> 00:43:17.883
in the two kinds of
treatments, am I wrong?

00:43:18.916 --> 00:43:21.330
- But eventually the
decision remains the same,

00:43:21.330 --> 00:43:23.820
whether you know about it
before you talk to these people

00:43:23.820 --> 00:43:26.550
or afterwards, the decision
that you have to take

00:43:26.550 --> 00:43:29.703
as a group is the same, the
incentives are the same,

00:43:31.050 --> 00:43:33.153
the financial incentives are the same.

00:43:34.260 --> 00:43:35.610
So the difference is whether

00:43:35.610 --> 00:43:38.366
you figure this out
before you talk to people

00:43:38.366 --> 00:43:39.720
and whether that changes the types

00:43:39.720 --> 00:43:42.870
of communications they
have with one another

00:43:42.870 --> 00:43:45.603
or whether you only
realize this afterwards.

00:43:46.650 --> 00:43:47.670
- [Dmitri] But the deliberation treatment

00:43:47.670 --> 00:43:50.270
is the treatment without
the payoffs, is that right?

00:43:51.701 --> 00:43:52.711
- Same payoffs.
- Same payoffs.

00:43:52.711 --> 00:43:54.420
So there's some aspect I'm missing.

00:43:54.420 --> 00:43:55.410
So what's the difference between

00:43:55.410 --> 00:43:56.730
the strategic deliberation treatment

00:43:56.730 --> 00:43:58.980
and the deliberation treatment?

00:43:58.980 --> 00:44:00.660
- The only difference is that you know it

00:44:00.660 --> 00:44:02.940
before you enter the Zoom room.

00:44:02.940 --> 00:44:05.520
So we tell you everything
about this election

00:44:05.520 --> 00:44:08.350
that you will have about a policy option

00:44:09.360 --> 00:44:11.520
before you go into the Zoom room

00:44:11.520 --> 00:44:13.140
in the strategic treatment.

00:44:13.140 --> 00:44:15.134
Whereas we only tell you this

00:44:15.134 --> 00:44:16.470
after you've been in the Zoom room

00:44:16.470 --> 00:44:20.193
in the normal just regular
deliberation treatments.

00:44:21.300 --> 00:44:22.830
In terms of financial incentives

00:44:22.830 --> 00:44:25.330
and payoffs, et cetera,
everything stays the same.

00:44:26.558 --> 00:44:29.160
And we also have the control
group, same incentives.

00:44:29.160 --> 00:44:31.829
The only difference is
they don't talk at all.

00:44:31.829 --> 00:44:32.662
- [Dmitri] So what was your explanation

00:44:32.662 --> 00:44:36.453
for the differences in the
arguments that you're seeing?

00:44:37.350 --> 00:44:41.670
- Well we need more support
from measuring the mechanism,

00:44:41.670 --> 00:44:43.980
but I am buying into this idea that

00:44:43.980 --> 00:44:45.300
deliberation is going on

00:44:45.300 --> 00:44:49.440
and there is an exchange
of views and people either

00:44:49.440 --> 00:44:51.780
spend more attention on something,

00:44:51.780 --> 00:44:53.130
maybe they're really persuaded,

00:44:53.130 --> 00:44:55.230
even though they don't
tell us they're persuaded

00:44:55.230 --> 00:44:59.580
in exit surveys, or maybe
it's the information

00:44:59.580 --> 00:45:01.080
that we're represented with

00:45:01.080 --> 00:45:03.430
and that they have
processed in a different way

00:45:04.530 --> 00:45:07.230
when the group is in a normal

00:45:07.230 --> 00:45:09.540
deliberation environment
versus a strategic one

00:45:09.540 --> 00:45:11.400
where they know this
will have consequences

00:45:11.400 --> 00:45:13.713
for the payoffs and the experiments.

00:45:15.630 --> 00:45:17.280
- [Ali] Very quickly.

00:45:17.280 --> 00:45:19.803
- [Audience Member] Sorry, would you just,

00:45:21.093 --> 00:45:21.990
your answer here about
strategic interaction

00:45:21.990 --> 00:45:24.450
made me think about the Denmark example.

00:45:24.450 --> 00:45:28.350
So a question I was sort of left with,

00:45:28.350 --> 00:45:31.740
and maybe we don't know enough about it,

00:45:31.740 --> 00:45:34.650
why did they divide up into five groups?

00:45:34.650 --> 00:45:36.750
It didn't seem like they were specialized

00:45:36.750 --> 00:45:41.750
committees, they were
debating the entire document

00:45:41.820 --> 00:45:43.080
in each group.

00:45:43.080 --> 00:45:46.920
And what's interesting is if you imagine,

00:45:46.920 --> 00:45:49.650
if you can control the
deliberation of one group,

00:45:49.650 --> 00:45:51.540
who is the decision maker,

00:45:51.540 --> 00:45:53.790
then all of a sudden the
stakes are very different

00:45:53.790 --> 00:45:56.956
compared to if there are four other groups

00:45:56.956 --> 00:45:58.756
who are all doing the same decision.

00:46:01.825 --> 00:46:02.970
That changes the strategy

00:46:02.970 --> 00:46:04.383
because being decisive,

00:46:05.460 --> 00:46:07.950
steering your group to
one decisive conclusion

00:46:07.950 --> 00:46:09.930
would be different than for other ones.

00:46:09.930 --> 00:46:11.070
So basically what I'm thinking

00:46:11.070 --> 00:46:14.040
of is why don't you try
to mimic the structure

00:46:14.040 --> 00:46:15.693
of the Denmark example?

00:46:17.550 --> 00:46:18.550
It goes beyond this.

00:46:21.150 --> 00:46:22.500
If you're trying to test some

00:46:22.500 --> 00:46:24.780
of these strategic mechanisms,

00:46:24.780 --> 00:46:28.050
it seems like the context having

00:46:28.050 --> 00:46:30.540
a very different structure might have some

00:46:30.540 --> 00:46:32.160
of these knock on effects
when you think about

00:46:32.160 --> 00:46:34.470
the other groups who are also deliberating

00:46:34.470 --> 00:46:35.520
on the same question.

00:46:38.280 --> 00:46:40.530
- That's one possibility
and an important one.

00:46:40.530 --> 00:46:43.110
And that's why I also wanted
to really present a framework

00:46:43.110 --> 00:46:44.460
because it really fits into that.

00:46:44.460 --> 00:46:48.360
I think it's also important
to keep the research on topic.

00:46:48.360 --> 00:46:50.490
Like it matters because today we're trying

00:46:50.490 --> 00:46:53.670
to get good outcomes from
these deliberative groups.

00:46:53.670 --> 00:46:55.500
We don't have to necessarily assume

00:46:55.500 --> 00:46:57.090
that they will be manipulated,

00:46:57.090 --> 00:47:00.180
but we can learn from these past cases

00:47:00.180 --> 00:47:03.010
and find out whether
maybe sometimes they were

00:47:04.686 --> 00:47:05.519
or what the reasons were

00:47:05.519 --> 00:47:07.260
why they were perhaps less successful

00:47:07.260 --> 00:47:09.570
than some of the other examples we had,

00:47:09.570 --> 00:47:11.490
and to really understand the context

00:47:11.490 --> 00:47:16.113
and how it influences the
quality of deliberation.

00:47:19.064 --> 00:47:19.912
- [Ali] Thank you very much.

00:47:19.912 --> 00:47:22.162
(applause)

